1 OCCU Slektsforsker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Stian Herlofsen Finne-Grønn (31 August 1869 – 1 November 1963) was a Norwegian genealogist.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Risør as the son of engineer Samuel Grønn (1838–1898) and Jacobine Finne (1845–1912). In October 1900 he married Margrethe Borchgrevink (1873–1963), a daughter of Sofie Borchgrevink.
2 CONT
2 CONT He finished his secondary education in 1888, and after a some years where he studied and worked as an architect, he enrolled in law studies. He graduated with the cand.jur. degree in 1899. His first genealogical work was about lin
2 CONC eages in his native Risør, and was published in several volumes between 1895 and 1901. He was a secretary in Statistics Norway from 1900 to 1907, then worked in the National Archival Services of Norway from 1907 to 1914. From 1914
2 CONC  to 1939 he worked in the archival services of Oslo city.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1906 he created the periodical Norsk tidsskrift for genealogi, personalhistorie, biografi og litteraturhistorie, which was Norway's first periodical on genealogy. Editors were Finne-Grønn, Erik Andreas Thomle and Christoffer Mo
2 CONC rgenstierne Munthe.[2] Norway did not have a national genealogical society, rather there was a Dano-Norwegian organization Samfundet for dansk-norsk Genealogi og Personalhistorie. Finne-Grønn chaired the Norwegian branch from 1919
2 CONC  to 1920.[1] In 1926 Finne-Grønn founded the Norwegian Genealogical Society together with Munthe and Sigurd Segelcke Meidell, among others.[2] He was the first chairman, from 1926 to 1929,[3] and as the organization launched a new
2 CONC  periodical Norsk Slektshistorisk Tidsskrift, Finne-Grønn edited it from 1926 to 1936 and 1947 to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also founded what is today known as Oslo City Museum, which developed from the organization Foreningen Det gamle Christiania. He became curator of this organization in 1912, and was the director from 1920 to 1949.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finne Grønn was decorated as a Knight, First Class of the Order of St. Olav in 1945. In 1942 he was created an honorary member of the Norwegian Genealogical Society and the Genealogical Society of Finland. A Festchrift was issued 
2 CONC for his seventy-fifth birthday. He died in November 1963 in Oslo.
